1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,754
This little clay jar is about as tall as your hand.

2
00:00:03,754 --> 00:00:07,108
It was dug up near Baghdad in nineteen thirty six.

3
00:00:07,108 --> 00:00:11,199
And for almost ninety years, people have called it a battery.

4
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:14,393
Two thousand years old, the story goes.

5
00:00:14,393 --> 00:00:19,566
Electricity eighteen centuries before Volta's battery of eighteen hundred.

6
00:00:19,566 --> 00:00:24,600
Proof, some say, of a lost science, or of knowledge from somewhere else.

7
00:00:25,066 --> 00:00:27,458
Inside it there is a copper tube.

8
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:29,560
Inside the tube, an iron rod.

9
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,127
Add an acid, and two different metals really do make a current.

10
00:00:34,127 --> 00:00:35,866
That part is not a myth.

11
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:39,119
So today we are not going to argue about it.

12
00:00:39,119 --> 00:00:46,399
We are going to put it on the bench, take it apart like any other machine, and run every test anyone has run on it.

13
00:00:50,866 --> 00:00:51,615
Four parts.

14
00:00:51,615 --> 00:00:52,773
Five bench tests.

15
00:00:52,773 --> 00:00:54,612
The parts that are missing.

16
00:00:54,612 --> 00:00:56,859
Then a spec sheet, and a verdict.

17
00:00:56,859 --> 00:01:00,333
Was it a battery at all, or something much quieter?

18
00:01:00,799 --> 00:01:01,743
Intake first.

19
00:01:01,743 --> 00:01:03,701
Who brought this object in?

20
00:01:03,701 --> 00:01:11,900
Wilhelm König, an Austrian painter who in the nineteen thirties ran the laboratory of the Iraq Museum in Baghdad.

21
00:01:12,366 --> 00:01:19,738
The jar came from Khujut Rabu, just outside Baghdad, near the ruins of the old capital Ctesiphon.

22
00:01:19,738 --> 00:01:23,538
One report says it turned up during railway works.

23
00:01:23,538 --> 00:01:25,666
The dig was barely recorded.

24
00:01:26,133 --> 00:01:34,936
In nineteen thirty eight König published a short note with a question mark in the title: a galvanic element from the Parthian era?

25
00:01:34,936 --> 00:01:39,000
He wondered if ancient gilded silver had been electroplated.

26
00:01:39,466 --> 00:01:43,548
A year later the idea jumped to a science fiction magazine.

27
00:01:43,548 --> 00:01:48,529
Astounding ran the headline: an electric battery two thousand years ago.

28
00:01:48,529 --> 00:01:50,466
It never stopped travelling.

29
00:01:50,933 --> 00:01:53,430
Ancient astronaut books went further.

30
00:01:53,430 --> 00:01:58,425
Maybe these batteries powered the so called light bulbs carved at Dendera.

31
00:01:58,425 --> 00:02:01,733
That claim has its own case file on this channel.

32
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:06,511
Others wired it into the Ark of the Covenant as a machine.

33
00:02:06,511 --> 00:02:08,220
Again, a separate file.

34
00:02:08,220 --> 00:02:15,133
Today we test only the jar, because every bigger claim stands or falls on this one small pot.

35
00:02:15,599 --> 00:02:16,291
Part one.

36
00:02:16,291 --> 00:02:16,906
The jar.

37
00:02:16,906 --> 00:02:23,513
Pale yellow clay, unglazed, thirteen to fifteen centimetres high, its neck broken off.

38
00:02:23,513 --> 00:02:25,664
Nothing about it is unusual.

39
00:02:25,664 --> 00:02:28,199
It is ordinary household pottery.

40
00:02:28,666 --> 00:02:31,525
And ordinary pottery tells us its age.

41
00:02:31,525 --> 00:02:37,392
König called it Parthian, the empire that ruled here from about two fifty B.C.

42
00:02:37,392 --> 00:02:37,919
to A.D.

43
00:02:37,919 --> 00:02:39,122
two twenty four.

44
00:02:39,122 --> 00:02:42,733
That is where the two thousand years comes from.

45
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:52,196
But St John Simpson of the British Museum said the grounds for a Parthian date are unclear, and the pot's style is Sasanian.

46
00:02:52,196 --> 00:02:55,896
That makes it a few centuries younger, roughly A.D.

47
00:02:55,896 --> 00:02:58,000
two twenty five to six forty.

48
00:02:58,466 --> 00:03:02,569
Either way it is genuinely old, and genuinely from Iraq.

49
00:03:02,569 --> 00:03:04,474
The first part checks out.

50
00:03:04,474 --> 00:03:05,866
Now let us open it.

51
00:03:06,333 --> 00:03:06,985
Part two.

52
00:03:06,985 --> 00:03:08,145
The copper tube.

53
00:03:08,145 --> 00:03:13,943
Rolled from sheet copper, twenty six millimetres wide and nine centimetres tall.

54
00:03:13,943 --> 00:03:18,800
Fairly pure copper, König said, with traces of zinc, lead and iron.

55
00:03:19,266 --> 00:03:25,395
Its bottom is closed with a copper disc, soldered with lead and covered with more bitumen.

56
00:03:25,395 --> 00:03:27,574
The tube was built to be sealed.

57
00:03:27,574 --> 00:03:28,800
Hold that thought.

58
00:03:29,266 --> 00:03:31,754
Now the first engineering problem.

59
00:03:31,754 --> 00:03:35,193
A battery needs two terminals to attach a wire.

60
00:03:35,193 --> 00:03:38,046
The iron rod sticks up out of the plug.

61
00:03:38,046 --> 00:03:40,680
But the copper is buried in asphalt.

62
00:03:40,680 --> 00:03:43,900
No tab, nowhere obvious to connect anything.

63
00:03:44,366 --> 00:03:49,396
A modern dry cell looks a little like this: a metal cup around a central rod.

64
00:03:49,396 --> 00:03:52,531
That is probably why König thought of a battery.

65
00:03:52,531 --> 00:03:57,300
But looking like a battery and being wired like one are different things.

66
00:03:57,766 --> 00:03:58,598
Part three.

67
00:03:58,598 --> 00:04:02,077
The iron rod, found completely rusted through.

68
00:04:02,077 --> 00:04:05,708
Battery fans say that is what acid does to iron.

69
00:04:05,708 --> 00:04:10,699
Popular articles even claim tests found traces of vinegar or wine.

70
00:04:11,166 --> 00:04:14,914
But trace that vinegar claim back and it runs out.

71
00:04:14,914 --> 00:04:17,987
No source, no lab, no published analysis.

72
00:04:17,987 --> 00:04:23,833
An iron rod buried for centuries in Iraqi soil will rust with or without acid.

73
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,341
Then the shape.

74
00:04:25,341 --> 00:04:33,468
The chemist Emmerich Paszthory found that an iron electrode eats away fastest at the neck, where it meets the liquid.

75
00:04:33,468 --> 00:04:35,205
The ancient rod does not.

76
00:04:35,205 --> 00:04:37,566
It is simply tapered, like a nail.

77
00:04:38,033 --> 00:04:38,776
Part four.

78
00:04:38,776 --> 00:04:39,444
The seal.

79
00:04:39,444 --> 00:04:45,760
Bitumen, natural asphalt, used in Mesopotamia for everything from boats to jewellery.

80
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:50,366
Here it plugs the copper tube and holds the iron rod in place.

81
00:04:50,833 --> 00:04:54,279
And here the battery idea gets into real trouble.

82
00:04:54,279 --> 00:05:00,258
Chemists found that a copper iron cell with fruit acid only runs while it has oxygen.

83
00:05:00,258 --> 00:05:05,533
Seal the tube airtight, like this one, and the current dies almost at once.

84
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,134
Bitumen also softens with heat.

85
00:05:08,134 --> 00:05:12,748
To top up the liquid you would have to dig out the plug every time.

86
00:05:12,748 --> 00:05:15,502
A great way to close something for good.

87
00:05:15,502 --> 00:05:17,500
A bad way to build a battery.

88
00:05:17,966 --> 00:05:19,724
So that is the teardown.

89
00:05:19,724 --> 00:05:25,435
A real ancient jar, a sealed copper tube, a rusted iron rod, an airtight plug.

90
00:05:25,435 --> 00:05:26,826
Every part is real.

91
00:05:26,826 --> 00:05:29,389
The question is what they were for.

92
00:05:29,389 --> 00:05:31,366
Time to put it under power.

93
00:05:31,833 --> 00:05:32,911
Bench test one.

94
00:05:32,911 --> 00:05:34,779
Pittsfield, Massachusetts.

95
00:05:34,779 --> 00:05:43,258
After the magazine story, Willard Gray, an engineer at General Electric, built the first replica with copper sulphate.

96
00:05:43,258 --> 00:05:47,066
It worked, he reported, quite well, for a short time.

97
00:05:47,533 --> 00:05:48,587
Bench test two.

98
00:05:48,587 --> 00:05:57,512
Students at Smith College, with historian of science Marjorie Senechal, measured between zero point eight and nearly two volts.

99
00:05:57,512 --> 00:06:01,800
Her view: they may have been batteries, because they do work.

100
00:06:02,266 --> 00:06:04,596
Bench test three, the famous one.

101
00:06:04,596 --> 00:06:07,701
Hildesheim, Germany, nineteen seventy eight.

102
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:17,866
Museum director Arne Eggebrecht linked replicas filled with grape juice and announced a silver plating one ten thousandth of a millimetre thick.

103
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:23,166
Decades later a researcher at the same museum searched for the records.

104
00:06:23,166 --> 00:06:29,088
There is no written documentation of those experiments, she said, not even photographs.

105
00:06:29,088 --> 00:06:29,633
Nothing.

106
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:33,429
The chemist Gerhard Eggert added two details.

107
00:06:33,429 --> 00:06:38,386
The replica shown to journalists was open, with no asphalt stopper.

108
00:06:38,386 --> 00:06:43,787
And for television, a museum restorer in a white coat posed as a chemist.

109
00:06:43,787 --> 00:06:44,897
Good publicity.

110
00:06:44,897 --> 00:06:45,933
Weak evidence.

111
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:47,598
Bench test four.

112
00:06:47,598 --> 00:06:56,433
In March two thousand five, the MythBusters build team made ten replica jars by hand and filled them with lemon juice.

113
00:06:56,899 --> 00:07:02,744
In series they gave four point three three volts, about a third of a volt per jar.

114
00:07:02,744 --> 00:07:06,593
Overnight, that coated a small copper token with zinc.

115
00:07:06,593 --> 00:07:10,299
Five jars could be felt through acupuncture needles.

116
00:07:10,766 --> 00:07:15,433
They rated plating, medicine and religious tingles all plausible.

117
00:07:15,433 --> 00:07:16,294
Fair enough.

118
00:07:16,294 --> 00:07:20,243
Plausible means it can be done, not that anyone did it.

119
00:07:20,243 --> 00:07:25,700
The archaeologist Ken Feder noted the gap: nobody has found the connections.

120
00:07:26,166 --> 00:07:28,320
Bench test five is brand new.

121
00:07:28,320 --> 00:07:42,133
In January twenty twenty six, independent researcher Alexander Bazes argued that the porous jar and the tin in the solder form a second cell, a tin air battery, in series with the first.

122
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:47,927
His replicas passed one point four volts, enough to plate and split water.

123
00:07:47,927 --> 00:07:53,543
But they used strong lye, in a paper series that welcomes unconventional work.

124
00:07:53,543 --> 00:07:55,631
It shows a design could work.

125
00:07:55,631 --> 00:07:58,800
It still finds no wire and no plated object.

126
00:07:59,266 --> 00:08:01,772
So the bench tests prove one thing.

127
00:08:01,772 --> 00:08:04,993
Copper, iron and an acid make a weak current.

128
00:08:04,993 --> 00:08:06,712
That was never in doubt.

129
00:08:06,712 --> 00:08:11,866
None of them can tell us what ancient Iraqis actually did with this jar.

130
00:08:12,333 --> 00:08:17,824
Which brings us to the most important part of any teardown: the parts that are missing.

131
00:08:17,824 --> 00:08:21,233
A working power source needs a whole system around it.

132
00:08:21,699 --> 00:08:22,676
First, wires.

133
00:08:22,676 --> 00:08:25,081
One jar gives well under a volt.

134
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:29,664
Anything useful needs several in a row, linked by conductors.

135
00:08:29,664 --> 00:08:35,000
Not one wire, strap or connector has been found with any of these jars.

136
00:08:35,466 --> 00:08:45,566
British Museum metallurgist Paul Craddock said it plainly: it is a pity we have not found any wires, because our interpretation could be completely wrong.

137
00:08:46,033 --> 00:08:47,701
Second, plated objects.

138
00:08:47,701 --> 00:08:52,415
If plating was the point, we should find electroplated metalwork.

139
00:08:52,415 --> 00:08:53,141
We do not.

140
00:08:53,141 --> 00:08:59,233
Gilding from this region and era used gold foil, gold leaf and mercury fire gilding.

141
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:05,009
Mercury gilding leaves mercury in the gold, which is how conservators spot it.

142
00:09:05,009 --> 00:09:12,633
Getty scientist David Scott wrote there is absolutely no evidence for electroplating in this region at the time.

143
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:15,140
Third, chemical fingerprints.

144
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:21,051
A jar run as a battery should be stained with copper salts, with copper on the iron.

145
00:09:21,051 --> 00:09:23,233
None of that has been reported.

146
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:25,311
And König's silversmiths?

147
00:09:25,311 --> 00:09:33,500
He had watched Baghdad jewellers gilding with a clay pot and a piece of zinc, and guessed it was an ancient secret passed down.

148
00:09:33,966 --> 00:09:35,148
Eggert traced it.

149
00:09:35,148 --> 00:09:43,560
It is almost exactly a method invented in Birmingham, England, in eighteen thirty nine by John Wright, using a flowerpot.

150
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:47,245
When König saw it, it was not two thousand years old.

151
00:09:47,245 --> 00:09:48,566
It was ninety nine.

152
00:09:49,033 --> 00:09:50,113
Now the twist.

153
00:09:50,113 --> 00:09:53,814
This jar has cousins, and they were found first.

154
00:09:53,814 --> 00:10:02,066
In nineteen thirty, at Seleucia on the Tigris, a University of Michigan team dug up four similar clay jars.

155
00:10:02,533 --> 00:10:08,463
Sealed with bitumen, three lay on their sides, pinned with bronze and iron rods.

156
00:10:08,463 --> 00:10:11,280
Inside each, a sealed bronze cylinder.

157
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:16,766
Inside those: plant fibres, and in one, what looked like a rolled papyrus.

158
00:10:17,233 --> 00:10:20,722
Nearby were magic bowls, made to trap demons.

159
00:10:20,722 --> 00:10:26,536
Two, covered in imitation writing, were stacked over an inscribed eggshell.

160
00:10:26,536 --> 00:10:31,033
The Baghdad jar was also reported with magic bowls nearby.

161
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:38,722
In nineteen thirty one, at neighbouring Ctesiphon, a German American expedition found six more.

162
00:10:38,722 --> 00:10:42,675
Some held sealed bronze rolls around decayed fibres.

163
00:10:42,675 --> 00:10:46,933
Others, lead plates, or ten iron nails wrapped in fibre.

164
00:10:47,399 --> 00:10:56,028
The excavator, Ernst Kühnel, called them containers for conjurations, blessings and the like, written perhaps on papyrus.

165
00:10:56,028 --> 00:10:57,027
Not batteries.

166
00:10:57,027 --> 00:10:59,166
Little sealed safes for words.

167
00:10:59,633 --> 00:11:03,478
Paszthory argued the whole group were magic objects.

168
00:11:03,478 --> 00:11:09,319
Copper was protective, and nails were used to pin things down, spells included.

169
00:11:09,319 --> 00:11:14,200
Magic nails survive in large numbers across the ancient Near East.

170
00:11:14,666 --> 00:11:19,779
Put the Baghdad jar back in that family and every odd feature makes sense.

171
00:11:19,779 --> 00:11:22,888
A sealed tube is a perfect case for a scroll.

172
00:11:22,888 --> 00:11:25,238
The airtight bitumen is the point.

173
00:11:25,238 --> 00:11:28,899
And a rod to fix it is exactly what the cousins have.

174
00:11:29,366 --> 00:11:31,766
That is why the field has moved on.

175
00:11:31,766 --> 00:11:40,266
In twenty twelve the Iraq specialist Elizabeth Stone said: I don't know anybody who thinks it's a real battery in the field.

176
00:11:40,733 --> 00:11:43,254
One idea deserves a fair hearing.

177
00:11:43,254 --> 00:11:47,302
Paul Keyser suggested a weak battery for pain relief.

178
00:11:47,302 --> 00:11:51,962
But the ancients already had natural electricity: around A.D.

179
00:11:51,962 --> 00:11:59,066
forty seven, the Roman doctor Scribonius Largus prescribed a live electric ray for headaches.

180
00:11:59,533 --> 00:12:06,038
Craddock even imagined priests hiding batteries in a statue to give worshippers a tingle.

181
00:12:06,038 --> 00:12:06,842
A fun idea.

182
00:12:06,842 --> 00:12:09,766
But no wired statue has ever been found.

183
00:12:10,233 --> 00:12:12,871
And we cannot re-test the original.

184
00:12:12,871 --> 00:12:19,126
Since the two thousand three invasion of Iraq, most of these artifacts are missing.

185
00:12:19,126 --> 00:12:24,100
One Ctesiphon jar survives at the Metropolitan Museum in New York.

186
00:12:24,566 --> 00:12:25,693
Teardown complete.

187
00:12:25,693 --> 00:12:27,133
Here is the spec sheet.

188
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:30,084
A genuine ancient jar: evidence.

189
00:12:30,084 --> 00:12:33,268
A replica makes a weak current: evidence.

190
00:12:33,268 --> 00:12:36,917
Wires, terminals or a plating cell: none found.

191
00:12:36,917 --> 00:12:40,566
Electroplated objects from the era: none found.

192
00:12:41,033 --> 00:12:44,405
Alien or lost high technology: no evidence.

193
00:12:44,405 --> 00:12:50,129
A sealed container for a scroll or spell, like its cousins: the best fit.

194
00:12:50,129 --> 00:12:53,266
Ever used as a battery at all: unproven.

195
00:12:53,733 --> 00:12:54,572
The verdict.

196
00:12:54,572 --> 00:13:00,658
The Baghdad Battery can make electricity, because almost any two metals in vinegar can.

197
00:13:00,658 --> 00:13:02,967
But there is no sign it ever did.

198
00:13:02,967 --> 00:13:09,333
The evidence points to a sealed jar for words, a blessing or a curse, meant to stay closed.

199
00:13:09,799 --> 00:13:19,066
And as Eggert put it, if aliens had brought real power technology, they could have left something far better than a cell that chokes on its own seal.

200
00:13:19,533 --> 00:13:22,050
What stays open is small and human.

201
00:13:22,050 --> 00:13:24,280
Whose words were sealed inside?

202
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:26,078
A protection for a house?

203
00:13:26,078 --> 00:13:26,726
A prayer?

204
00:13:26,726 --> 00:13:30,466
Those scrolls have rotted, and the original is lost.

205
00:13:30,933 --> 00:13:33,939
But its cousins survive in museum cases.

206
00:13:33,939 --> 00:13:41,305
A reminder that the most interesting thing inside an ancient object is usually not a power source.

207
00:13:41,305 --> 00:13:42,433
It is a person.

208
00:13:42,899 --> 00:13:44,252
That is the teardown.

209
00:13:44,252 --> 00:13:47,084
Battery or scroll jar, what is your verdict?

210
00:13:47,084 --> 00:13:52,299
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient machine on the bench.
